  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Eighty sheep were shipped to a Scottish island in 1942. Gruinard, in Gruinard Bay, a kilometre off the Wester Ross coast.",
        "visual_query": "scottish highland island rocky coast",
        "visual_fallback": "rocky island coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Porton Down scientists detonated anthrax bombs beside tethered groups of them. The sheep began dying within days. The bombs were part of Operation Vegetarian.",
        "visual_query": "wartime 1940s military field test",
        "visual_fallback": "wartime archive"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The plan was never used on anyone, and the cakes were destroyed. So why did the island stay shut for nearly half a century?",
        "visual_query": "deserted scottish island windswept grass",
        "visual_fallback": "windswept grass"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Anthrax spores survive in soil for decades. Warning signs went up, visits were banned, and only inspectors landed to test the ground.",
        "visual_query": "weathered warning sign coastal shore",
        "visual_fallback": "warning sign"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1986 crews soaked the island in 280 tonnes of formaldehyde. A test flock stayed healthy, and the signs came down in April 1990.",
        "visual_query": "sheep grazing scottish island hillside",
        "visual_fallback": "grazing sheep"
      }
    ],
